WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:01.968 line:0%
-Jesse.
-Hello.

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:04.103 line:0%
-Oh, thank you so much
for coming on.

00:00:04.103 --> 00:00:05.471 line:0%
I really appreciate this.

00:00:05.471 --> 00:00:07.406 line:0%
-Thank you so much for
having me,

00:00:07.406 --> 00:00:08.841 line:0%
you know, on the computer.

00:00:08.841 --> 00:00:12.678 line:0%
-Yeah. You did something that
I've dreamt of doing

00:00:12.678 --> 00:00:15.315 line:0%
my whole life, except you did it
during a pandemic.

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:17.917 line:0%
You took an RV trip.
I'd love to know about this.

00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:19.118 line:0%
-Yes, yes.

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:21.187 line:0%
I mean, it was out of
necessity, not, like,

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:23.255 line:0%
any kind of, you know,
dream or whim.

00:00:23.255 --> 00:00:25.759 line:0%
It was like we were, like,
stuck in Los Angeles

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:27.359 line:0%
when, you know,
the world shut down,

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:30.529 line:0%
and we had to get back to
Indiana, where we live.

00:00:30.529 --> 00:00:32.465 line:0%
And so, yeah, we rented an RV so
we could kind of, like,

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:35.601 line:0%
quarantine safely and also move.

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:38.104 line:0%
-And, obviously,
you took the baby with you.

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:40.139 line:0%
-Yeah.
-Did he like it?

00:00:40.139 --> 00:00:42.241 line:0%
Was he like, "What's going on?"

00:00:42.241 --> 00:00:45.045 line:0%
-Well, he's 3. He's almost 4.

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:46.378 line:0%
I just asked him today,
"Do you remember

00:00:46.378 --> 00:00:48.615 line:0%
when we spent that amazing week
in the camper van?"

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:50.816 line:0%
He said, "No."
And I showed him a picture.

00:00:50.816 --> 00:00:53.552 line:0%
He's like, "No.
Put on 'Paw Patrol.' Enough."

00:00:53.552 --> 00:00:55.120 line:0%
-I said, "This was, like,
the best week of your life."

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.522 line:0%
I showed him pictures.

00:00:56.522 --> 00:00:58.825 line:0%
I said, "You said this was
the best thing in your life."

00:00:58.825 --> 00:00:59.926 line:0%
He's like,
"No, I don't remember that.

00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:01.160 line:0%
I don't remember it at all."

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:02.561 line:0%
-I've had that conversation
before, too, yeah.

00:01:02.561 --> 00:01:04.497 line:0%
-I'm wondering,
do we have to do anything nice

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:05.998 line:0%
for our children before 7?

00:01:05.998 --> 00:01:07.666 line:0%
They're not gonna remember.
-No, 7 is the age.

00:01:07.666 --> 00:01:09.002 line:0%
That's when they start
remembering. Yeah.

00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:10.904 line:0%
-Really?
-Yeah. That's exactly it.

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:12.505 line:0%
They don't care.
They don't care at all.

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:13.639 line:0%
No, nothing until 7.

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:15.307 line:0%
-I might buy myself
stuff from now on.

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:18.244 line:0%
-Were you nervous to drive
that giant RV?

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:20.079 line:0%
-Yeah. I mean,
I was a little nervous.

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:22.248 line:0%
It's a bit of a learning curve
with the RV,

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:24.917 line:0%
and then, if you're kind of,
like, an idiot, like me,

00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:28.488 line:0%
you know, you feel cocky, like,
way too prematurely.

00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:30.289 line:0%
So, like, you know,
after like 20 minutes,

00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:31.590 line:0%
once I didn't get
into an accident,

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.826 line:0%
I started, you know, changing
lanes without signaling.

00:01:33.826 --> 00:01:35.962 line:0%
Yeah, exactly.
-One-hander. Yeah, yeah.

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:37.030 line:0%
Uh-huh.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.597 line:0%
You know, like
the idiot 7-year-old boy,

00:01:38.597 --> 00:01:41.000 line:0%
who, like, you know,
is on a bicycle for 20 seconds,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.368 line:0%
and he comes back
with a split lip

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:44.337 line:0%
because he decided to
pop a wheelie.

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:46.406 line:0%
-Yeah, with no hands.
-Exactly.

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:47.974 line:0%
-Did you get to meet
the communities?

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:51.077 line:0%
'Cause that's what I'm obsessed
about, too, like, the RV world.

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:53.079 line:0%
-It's a culture.
It's a whole culture.

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:54.280 line:0%
-It's fantastic.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.581 line:0%
-And the interesting thing is,

00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:57.883 line:0%
the RV culture does not end
at, like, the RV park.

00:01:57.883 --> 00:02:00.786 line:0%
Now, like, when I see an RV in
town, like, where I live,

00:02:00.786 --> 00:02:02.288 line:0%
you know, I kind of I give
the guy a nod,

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:05.057 line:0%
and he gives me a nod, and then
we usually discuss hitches.

00:02:05.057 --> 00:02:07.327 line:0%
You know, it's like a whole --

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:09.696 line:0%
And, like, everybody has
the same gripes, like,

00:02:09.696 --> 00:02:11.898 line:0%
"Why can't the Forester just
carry something bigger?"

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:14.333 line:0%
Like, everybody has the same
very specific gripes

00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:16.202 line:0%
that sound absurd
if you don't know the culture.

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:19.772 line:0%
-Yeah, I've done a tiny, little
RV trip, you know,

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:21.373 line:0%
10 minutes away
from my house just to see

00:02:21.373 --> 00:02:23.776 line:0%
if I would like it
in a campground.

00:02:23.776 --> 00:02:25.878 line:0%
And I did. I loved it.
I did it twice.

00:02:25.878 --> 00:02:27.313 line:0%
And I just wanted to be
closer to home --

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:29.515 line:0%
-You rented an RV for a
10-minute drive?

00:02:29.515 --> 00:02:31.350 line:0%
-Yeah. I didn't even actually
drive it there.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:32.718 line:0%
My friend drove it there
and parked it,

00:02:32.718 --> 00:02:34.219 line:0%
and then I went
and stayed there.

00:02:34.219 --> 00:02:35.955 line:0%
-Well, that's a
definite dry run.

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:38.324 line:0%
-Well, yeah, I just wanted to
make sure, you know,

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:40.626 line:0%
if I went out, I got out,
you know?

00:02:40.626 --> 00:02:44.130 line:0%
-And you feel...
-I loved it. I loved the people.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.265 line:0%
Everyone was so nice to me.
They were offering me things.

00:02:46.265 --> 00:02:48.167 line:0%
It felt so neighborly.

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:49.301 line:0%
And it was just -- There was

00:02:49.301 --> 00:02:51.971 line:0%
something kind of
beautiful about it.

00:02:51.971 --> 00:02:53.739 line:0%
It was a very interesting thing.

00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:55.908 line:0%
I thought I'd be like,
"I don't want to see people."

00:02:55.908 --> 00:02:56.976 line:0%
And I was the opposite.

00:02:56.976 --> 00:03:00.079 line:0%
I was like, "Hey,
I'll share this you.

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:02.081 line:0%
-I think it's because
we no longer --

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:03.616 line:0%
We're feel, like,
so infantilized in our

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:05.518 line:0%
normal lives, like,
in New York City, you know,

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:07.219 line:0%
'cause you don't, like,
fix anything.

00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:09.421 line:0%
And then, here, because you're
in charge of everything,

00:03:09.421 --> 00:03:11.491 line:0%
you feel, like, confident enough
to talk to people,

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:13.826 line:0%
like, you know, the way early
man used to talk to people,

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:15.661 line:0%
because you're not,
like, infantilized by,

00:03:15.661 --> 00:03:17.296 line:0%
you know, mass technology.

00:03:17.296 --> 00:03:19.298 line:0%
-Yeah. Did you get to
see the country

00:03:19.298 --> 00:03:21.234 line:0%
and take pictures
and all that stuff?

00:03:21.234 --> 00:03:22.969 line:0%
-Yeah. I mean,
it was totally surreal

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:25.238 line:0%
in the sense that, like --
So, you know, I mean,

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:27.173 line:0%
it's probably already
a surreal experience

00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:28.741 line:0%
because you're kind of, like,
on the "open road"

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:30.042 line:0%
or whatever
that's supposed to be,

00:03:30.042 --> 00:03:33.546 line:0%
but during a pandemic,
it was truly, like, empty.

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:36.149 line:0%
Like, we went to the
Grand Canyon, which was open,

00:03:36.149 --> 00:03:38.218 line:0%
and we were the only ones there.

00:03:38.218 --> 00:03:39.985 line:0%
Like, we went
to these amazing towns --

00:03:39.985 --> 00:03:42.321 line:0%
you know, Lawrence, Kansas,
St. Louis, Albuquerque,

00:03:42.321 --> 00:03:43.822 line:0%
and no one was there.

00:03:43.822 --> 00:03:46.959 line:0%
We would park -- Like, we would
park on the grounds of, like,

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:49.395 line:0%
a museum and we would,
like, walk around.

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:50.796 line:0%
It was like I was in "Big" or
"Honey, I Shrank the" --

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:52.097 line:0%
I don't know.

00:03:52.097 --> 00:03:54.533 line:0%
Whatever movie that conjures
that nice experience.

00:03:54.533 --> 00:03:56.736 line:0%
-This is a dream for me.
That's unbelievable.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:58.303 line:0%
That is unbelievable
that you did that.

00:03:58.303 --> 00:03:59.705 line:0%
I love that you did that.

00:03:59.705 --> 00:04:01.807 line:0%
-You're gonna have to wait
for another pandemic.

00:04:01.807 --> 00:04:04.444 line:0%
-You didn't freak out?
'Cause we've talked about this.

00:04:04.444 --> 00:04:06.979 line:0%
You're open about
dealing with anxiety.

00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:08.981 line:0%
Did you have anxiety
at all during --

00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:09.982 line:0%
Do you have it
now during the pandemic?

00:04:09.982 --> 00:04:11.417 line:0%
-No, no, no.

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:14.053 line:0%
I've been anxiety-free since
whenever the pandemic struck.

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:16.255 line:0%
Anytime there's
a real crisis in the world,

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.357 line:0%
I'm, like,
kind of an American hero.

00:04:18.357 --> 00:04:21.960 line:0%
364 days of the year,
American coward.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.863 line:0%
Crisis, American hero.

00:04:23.863 --> 00:04:25.831 line:0%
You know, because I'm in a panic

00:04:25.831 --> 00:04:27.266 line:0%
that something terrible
is going to happen.

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:29.635 line:0%
I mean, I think there's been
generations of me, you know,

00:04:29.635 --> 00:04:31.437 line:0%
in my lineage that are
in a panic,

00:04:31.437 --> 00:04:33.405 line:0%
waiting for
the bad thing to happen.

00:04:33.405 --> 00:04:36.008 line:0%
And so it makes us look really
out of step with society

00:04:36.008 --> 00:04:38.144 line:0%
and worried, but when something
actually happens,

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:39.311 line:0%
we're the only one
that's ready for it.

00:04:39.311 --> 00:04:40.613 line:0%
You know what I mean?

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:42.949 line:0%
Like, I carry a flare in my
back pocket just in case.

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:44.817 line:0%
It looks weird most of the time.

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:47.086 line:0%
But then, you know, if your car
gets -- Blow a tire --

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:50.089 line:0%
-A flare.

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:52.425 line:0%
Hey, can we talk about the
Audible Original?

00:04:52.425 --> 00:04:54.126 line:0%
I love that
people are doing this now.

00:04:54.126 --> 00:04:56.963 line:0%
This is the one thing I think is
kind of, you know,

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:59.198 line:0%
a little bit -- a cool thing
that's coming out

00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:01.667 line:0%
of this awful situation that
we're in is,

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:05.204 line:0%
everyone's getting very creative
and seeing what they can do

00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:08.374 line:0%
to just give people content
and help people.

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:12.712 line:0%
And any second of normalcy,
it just feels good for people.

00:05:12.712 --> 00:05:19.585 line:0%
You did this Audible, almost
like an Audible movie, kind of,

00:05:19.585 --> 00:05:21.387 line:0%
called "When You Finish
Saving the World."

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:23.956 line:0%
You wrote it. You star in it.
How would you describe this?

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:26.658 line:0%
-Yeah, it's essentially like a
kind of -- It's like a novel

00:05:26.658 --> 00:05:28.761 line:0%
told from the
three characters's perspective,

00:05:28.761 --> 00:05:29.828 line:0%
but it's all audio.

00:05:29.828 --> 00:05:32.698 line:0%
So, it would
be structured like a book

00:05:32.698 --> 00:05:35.667 line:0%
and it takes place over
the course of 30 years.

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:39.338 line:0%
I play a new father,
and then you jump 15 years

00:05:39.338 --> 00:05:43.409 line:0%
into the future, in 2032,
and Finn Wolfhard, the actor,

00:05:43.409 --> 00:05:44.910 line:0%
plays my son at 15.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:47.946 line:0%
And then you jump back 30 years,
to 2002,

00:05:47.946 --> 00:05:51.283 line:0%
and Kaitlyn Dever, the actress,
plays my wife

00:05:51.283 --> 00:05:52.518 line:0%
before we met.

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:54.220 line:0%
And so it's like this kind of a
story of a family

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:55.521 line:0%
over the course of 30 years,

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:58.191 line:0%
all centered in this town in
Bloomington, Indiana.

00:05:58.191 --> 00:06:00.593 line:0%
-I love that.
I cannot wait to listen to this.

00:06:00.593 --> 00:06:02.061 line:0%
This is fantastic.
-Thank you so much.

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:04.997 line:0%
-And you're already going to
make a film version about this?

00:06:04.997 --> 00:06:06.198 line:0%
Is that true?
-Yeah.

00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:08.334 line:0%
So, yeah, I adapted, like,
a small part of it

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:10.903 line:0%
and wrote a movie
that I'm going to direct

00:06:10.903 --> 00:06:13.873 line:0%
with Julianne Moore, and then,
this wonderful company, A24,

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:15.207 line:0%
is coming on to produce it.

00:06:15.207 --> 00:06:17.810 line:0%
So, we're, yeah,
about to leave for Manitoba,

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:20.746 line:0%
where there is, you know,
very little virus.

00:06:20.746 --> 00:06:22.548 line:0%
-I love A24, by the way.

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:24.283 line:0%
Anytime I see
that logo come up, I go,

00:06:24.283 --> 00:06:26.052 line:0%
"Oh, this movie's
going to be cool.

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:27.487 line:0%
-I know. I know.

00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:30.289 line:0%
I'm having trouble, like, just
because of my own pessimism,

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:31.890 line:0%
picturing the thing that
I'm worried I'm going to make

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:35.194 line:0%
coming after the logo that
I have a lot of confidence in.

00:06:35.194 --> 00:06:36.262 line:0%
-Well, I'm looking forward
to that

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:37.362 line:0%
and I'm looking forward
to listening

00:06:37.362 --> 00:06:39.265 line:0%
to your Audible Original.

00:06:39.265 --> 00:06:41.734 line:0%
It's always good to see you.
Stay safe.

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:45.438 line:0%
And, yeah, wait another four
years, then do another RV trip.

00:06:45.438 --> 00:06:46.506 line:0%
-Thanks.
I'll see you on the road.

00:06:46.506 --> 00:06:48.107 line:0%
We can give each other
a knowing nod.

00:06:48.107 --> 00:06:49.842 line:0%
-My thanks to Jesse Eisenberg.
See?

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:51.244 line:0%
I don't even have the nod down.
-I know, I know.

